  "content": {
    "id": "p62-q16-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": "Solve $3\\cos x-\\sqrt3\\sin x=2\\sqrt3$ for $0\\le x\\le2\\pi$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$$x=\\frac{11\\pi}6.$$\n\nWrite the left-hand side as $2\\sqrt3\\cos(x+\\frac\\pi6)$.",
      "worked": "Write $3\\cos x-\\sqrt3\\sin x=R\\cos(x+\\alpha)$, where $R>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}R&=\\sqrt{3^2+(\\sqrt3)^2}\\\\R&=2\\sqrt3.\\end{align*}$$\nMatching coefficients gives\n$$\\cos\\alpha=\\frac3{2\\sqrt3}=\\frac{\\sqrt3}2,\\qquad\\sin\\alpha=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2\\sqrt3}=\\frac12,$$\nso $\\alpha=\\frac\\pi6$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2\\sqrt3\\cos\\left(x+\\frac\\pi6\\right)&=2\\sqrt3\\\\\\cos\\left(x+\\frac\\pi6\\right)&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $0\\le x\\le2\\pi$, the angle $x+\\frac\\pi6$ lies in $[\\frac\\pi6,\\frac{13\\pi}6]$. The only angle in this interval with cosine $1$ is $2\\pi$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x+\\frac\\pi6&=2\\pi\\\\x&=\\frac{11\\pi}6.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
